1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,489
The syntactical world is governed by a deductive calculus,

2
00:00:03,589 --> 00:00:06,770
which we can visualize as a theorem-proving computer.

3
00:00:10,333 --> 00:00:14,883
The symbols of this system divide into logical and non-logical.

4
00:00:14,933 --> 00:00:18,455
The axioms divide in the same way. If we only feed in the

5
00:00:18,555 --> 00:00:24,175
so-called logical axioms --which determine the behaviour of the logical symbols-- into our

6
00:00:24,275 --> 00:00:27,607
theorem prover then we are in the world of pure logic.

7
00:00:27,666 --> 00:00:30,740
The outputs of our theorem prover will be logically

8
00:00:30,840 --> 00:00:33,540
valid formulas. We can think of this like the

9
00:00:33,640 --> 00:00:37,336
operating system, operating in the background of mathematics.

10
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:41,917
Then, to define a mathematical theory, such as group theory, or number

11
00:00:42,017 --> 00:00:46,402
theory, we will fix a set of constants, functions and relations, and

12
00:00:46,502 --> 00:00:50,491
add axioms which determine the behaviour of these new symbols.

13
00:00:50,533 --> 00:00:53,933
The choice of which symbols and axioms to include lead to

14
00:00:54,033 --> 00:00:57,740
different theories. And a theory is just the set of everything

15
00:00:57,840 --> 00:01:01,117
which can be outputted by the theorem proving computer.

16
00:01:01,733 --> 00:01:04,879
So, mathematical truth on the syntactic side is

17
00:01:04,979 --> 00:01:07,641
given by proofs in a deductive calculus.

18
00:01:07,733 --> 00:01:11,761
On the semantic side, truth is given by Tarski's definition: a

19
00:01:11,861 --> 00:01:15,023
sentence is true if and only if it holds in every

20
00:01:15,123 --> 00:01:18,685
model. Let us make this precise — it may feel technical

21
00:01:18,785 --> 00:01:21,948
for a moment, but it leads somewhere interesting.

22
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:27,451
Formally, a structure — which we denote with the symbol M —

23
00:01:27,551 --> 00:01:31,804
is given by a tuple consisting of a domain, or universe, together

24
00:01:31,904 --> 00:01:37,162
with interpretations of the constants, functions, and relations in the language.

25
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:40,899
To define the natural numbers with addition and multiplication, for

26
00:01:40,999 --> 00:01:43,905
instance, we need at least the symbols zero, one, the

27
00:01:44,005 --> 00:01:48,045
successor function, addition, multiplication, and the less-than relation.

28
00:01:48,133 --> 00:01:51,350
We interpret zero and one as expected.

29
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:55,739
The interpretation of functions and relations is a bit more abstract.

30
00:01:55,839 --> 00:01:59,858
For example, the less-than relation is interpreted as the set of

31
00:01:59,958 --> 00:02:02,689
all pairs (a, b) such that a is less than b.

32
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:07,000
Note that functions are just special relations where

33
00:02:07,100 --> 00:02:10,047
the output is uniquely determined by the inputs.

34
00:02:10,133 --> 00:02:13,783
So the successor function would map to the set of all pair

35
00:02:13,883 --> 00:02:17,791
of numbers such that the second is one greater than the first.

36
00:02:18,866 --> 00:02:22,876
And the addition function is formally the set of all triples

37
00:02:22,976 --> 00:02:25,480
(a, b, c) such that a plus b equals c.

38
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:30,219
To evaluate a simple equation like one plus two

39
00:02:30,319 --> 00:02:32,881
equals three, we map the constants as expected

40
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:35,325
and then check whether the resulting

41
00:02:35,425 --> 00:02:38,020
triple belongs to the addition relation.

